What does this program print? (Each call prints a word and a space.)

```cpp
#include <print>
#include <string>

void f(int) { std::print("int "); }
void f(double) { std::print("double "); }
void f(const std::string&) { std::print("string "); }

int main() {
    f('x');
    f(2.5f);
    f(true);
    f("hi");
    std::println("");
}
```

A colleague adds an overload, and now the program behaves differently:

```cpp
void show(bool b) { std::println("bool {}", b); }
void show(const std::string& s) { std::println("string {}", s); }

int main() { show("hello"); }
```

What does it print, and why?

What does this program print?

```cpp
#include <print>

int main() {
    int x = 10;
    auto by_value = [x] { return x + 1; };
    auto by_reference = [&x] { return x + 1; };
    x = 20;
    std::println("{} {}", by_value(), by_reference());
}
```

